1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,279
A cold Friday, in the garden of the royal
chancery. An old secretary has just

2
00:00:05,279 --> 00:00:10,107
carried the sultan's letters in and out, as he
has done for nearly thirty years. He

3
00:00:10,107 --> 00:00:14,819
sits down, and suddenly his body will not obey
him. His young assistant, who will

4
00:00:14,819 --> 00:00:17,320
one day write it all down, is not far away.

5
00:00:27,670 --> 00:00:32,867
Welcome to The Honest Historian. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani, and this is Folio Five. In the

6
00:00:32,867 --> 00:00:38,191
last four folios, you have heard about
viziers, princes, generals and caliphs. Today

7
00:00:38,191 --> 00:00:41,930
our hero holds no sword and wears no crown. He
holds a pen.

8
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:47,277
His name was Bu Nasr Moshkan. For nearly
thirty years he wrote the letters of two

9
00:00:47,277 --> 00:00:51,732
sultans. He knew their secrets, he guarded
their words, and he walked every day

10
00:00:51,732 --> 00:00:56,468
beside a throne that could kill him. And he
was the teacher of the man who wrote our

11
00:00:56,468 --> 00:00:56,750
book.

12
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:16,740
First, the place where he worked. Every great
court in medieval Iran had a set of

13
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:21,392
offices, called divans. One handled money. One
handled the army. And one handled

14
00:01:21,392 --> 00:01:26,277
letters. In Persian it was called the divan-e
resalat, the office of correspondence.

15
00:01:26,277 --> 00:01:28,370
We might call it the royal chancery.

16
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:33,958
This was where the sultan's words became
writing. Letters to the caliph in Baghdad.

17
00:01:33,958 --> 00:01:38,477
Letters to the khans of Central Asia. Orders
to governors, treaties, warnings,

18
00:01:38,477 --> 00:01:43,285
secret reports. Every one of them had to be
written in beautiful Persian or Arabic,

19
00:01:43,285 --> 00:01:44,270
sealed, and sent.

20
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:49,714
And every one of them, if it was wrong by a
single word, could start a war or end a

21
00:01:49,714 --> 00:01:50,150
career.

22
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:55,899
From around the year one thousand and eleven,
the head of this office at Ghazni was

23
00:01:55,899 --> 00:02:00,568
Bu Nasr Moshkan. His full name was Abu Nasr
Mansur, son of Moshkan. He ran the

24
00:02:00,568 --> 00:02:05,418
chancery for Sultan Mahmud, the great
conqueror, and then, after Mahmud died, for

25
00:02:05,418 --> 00:02:08,830
his son Masud, the sultan we met in Folios Two
and Three.

26
00:02:09,110 --> 00:02:14,263
Bu Nasr was a master of both languages.
Beyhaqi praised his writing again and again,

27
00:02:14,263 --> 00:02:19,170
and copied some of his letters into the
history, so that they would not be lost.

28
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:39,369
Around the year ten twenty, a young man from
the district of Beyhaq, in Khorasan,

29
00:02:39,369 --> 00:02:44,046
came to work in this office. He was about
twenty five years old. His name was

30
00:02:44,046 --> 00:02:46,050
Abolfazl. We know him as Beyhaqi.

31
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:51,532
Bu Nasr took him in as an assistant and a
pupil. Beyhaqi would work at his side for

32
00:02:51,532 --> 00:02:56,008
nineteen years. He watched how his master
wrote, how he spoke to kings, when he

33
00:02:56,008 --> 00:03:00,484
spoke, and above all when he stayed silent.
Beyhaqi calls him, again and again,

34
00:03:00,484 --> 00:03:03,090
ustadam, which means my master, or my teacher.

35
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:08,424
And much of what Beyhaqi tells us about the
court of Masud, he saw from his master's

36
00:03:08,424 --> 00:03:10,770
desk, or heard from his master's mouth.

37
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:16,753
So when we read Beyhaqi, we are often hearing
two voices. The pupil writes. But the

38
00:03:16,753 --> 00:03:19,010
teacher is standing just behind him.

39
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:38,897
Soon after Masud took the throne, in the year
ten thirty, the court was full of

40
00:03:38,897 --> 00:03:43,680
fear. The new sultan was bringing back an old
vizier, Ahmad Hasan, and men who had

41
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:48,404
served the old order wondered who would be
next to fall. Beyhaqi tells us plainly

42
00:03:48,404 --> 00:03:50,270
that his master was very afraid.

43
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:55,110
For a time, Bu Nasr stopped coming to sit in
the chancery at all.

44
00:03:55,890 --> 00:04:01,363
Masud sent for him. He spoke kindly to him,
praised him, and sent him back to work.

45
00:04:01,363 --> 00:04:06,770
Beyhaqi reports that the sultan said a man
like Bu Nasr was worth ten men to them.

46
00:04:07,550 --> 00:04:12,360
But at a court, praise draws enemies. A rival
named Bu Sahl Zawzani, a clever and

47
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:17,289
bitter man, went to the sultan with a
suggestion. Bu Nasr was rich, he said. If the

48
00:04:17,289 --> 00:04:22,158
sultan pressed him, three hundred thousand
dinars could be squeezed out of him for

49
00:04:22,158 --> 00:04:22,930
the treasury.

50
00:04:23,710 --> 00:04:28,810
Masud listened to the offer. And then he
answered. He refused. He would not ruin a

51
00:04:28,810 --> 00:04:29,930
servant like that.

52
00:04:30,710 --> 00:04:35,123
Then the sultan tested his secretary in
another way. He asked for his advice on

53
00:04:35,123 --> 00:04:39,536
great matters of state. And Bu Nasr hesitated.
He said he would rather not give

54
00:04:39,536 --> 00:04:44,116
advice, because others at court would call it
overreaching, a secretary playing at

55
00:04:44,116 --> 00:04:48,250
being a minister. Only when Masud insisted on
a frank answer did he speak.

56
00:04:49,030 --> 00:04:53,592
His advice was simple. Follow the path of your
late father, Mahmud, and do not speak

57
00:04:53,592 --> 00:04:57,882
ill of it. And keep the two armies united. By
this he meant the old soldiers of

58
00:04:57,882 --> 00:05:02,063
Mahmud and the new men who had come up with
Masud. If those two groups turned

59
00:05:02,063 --> 00:05:05,050
against each other, he warned, the kingdom
would split.

60
00:05:05,830 --> 00:05:10,110
It was good advice. As we will see, it was not
always followed.

61
00:05:25,390 --> 00:05:30,653
Now, do you remember Folio One, and Hasanak,
the vizier on the gallows? Bu Nasr was

62
00:05:30,653 --> 00:05:34,330
at court on that terrible day, in the year ten
thirty two.

63
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:39,844
Beyhaqi gives us one small detail about his
master that I have never forgotten. He

64
00:05:39,844 --> 00:05:44,521
says that on the day of the hanging, Bu Nasr
did not break his fast. He would not

65
00:05:44,521 --> 00:05:47,870
eat. He was deeply troubled, and he kept
saying one thing.

66
00:05:48,650 --> 00:05:50,450
Che omid manad?

67
00:05:51,230 --> 00:05:52,650
What hope is left?

68
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:58,027
Think about what that means. Bu Nasr could not
stop the hanging. Nobody could. If he

69
00:05:58,027 --> 00:06:02,460
had protested in public, he might have
followed Hasanak to the gallows. So he did

70
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:06,947
the only thing a careful man could do. He
refused to eat, and he grieved where his

71
00:06:06,947 --> 00:06:09,410
pupil could see. And his pupil wrote it down.

72
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:29,491
Now we jump forward to the year ten thirty
nine. The empire was in trouble. Nomad

73
00:06:29,491 --> 00:06:34,174
Turkmen horsemen, led by the Seljuq family,
had come out of the steppe and were

74
00:06:34,174 --> 00:06:39,035
raiding Khorasan, the rich eastern province of
Iran. Masud's army, huge and heavy,

75
00:06:39,035 --> 00:06:41,050
kept chasing them and kept losing.

76
00:06:41,330 --> 00:06:45,610
The summer was hot, the fodder ran out, and
the horses grew weak.

77
00:06:46,390 --> 00:06:51,490
One night, with the news getting worse and
worse, Masud sent for Bu Nasr, alone. Not

78
00:06:51,490 --> 00:06:56,104
the vizier, not the generals. The secretary.
And he asked him what he really

79
00:06:56,104 --> 00:06:56,590
thought.

80
00:06:57,370 --> 00:07:01,768
This was the most dangerous question a
courtier could be asked. Tell a proud king

81
00:07:01,768 --> 00:07:06,329
the truth, and you may lose his favor. Flatter
him, and you may lose the kingdom. Bu

82
00:07:06,329 --> 00:07:10,456
Nasr was quiet for a moment. And then,
carefully, he told the sultan what he

83
00:07:10,456 --> 00:07:10,890
thought.

84
00:07:11,670 --> 00:07:16,505
He said that these defeats looked to him like
a sign that God was displeased. He

85
00:07:16,505 --> 00:07:21,520
urged the sultan to pray, to repent, and to
make a vow that very night. And he used

86
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:22,850
an old Persian saying.

87
00:07:23,630 --> 00:07:26,850
Dowlat o mellat do baradar-and.

88
00:07:27,630 --> 00:07:32,412
The state and the faith are two brothers. In
other words, a ruler cannot be strong

89
00:07:32,412 --> 00:07:34,570
for long if he forgets what is right.

90
00:07:35,350 --> 00:07:40,241
Then he turned practical. An army dragging so
much baggage, he said, cannot fight

91
00:07:40,241 --> 00:07:44,950
the fast riders of the steppe. Separate the
fighting men from the heavy loads.

92
00:07:45,730 --> 00:07:50,463
Beyhaqi tells us that Masud accepted, and said
that he would do it. But later, Bu

93
00:07:50,463 --> 00:07:55,253
Nasr spoke to his pupil in private, and he was
close to despair. He said he wished

94
00:07:55,253 --> 00:07:58,350
they had died, rather than live to see such
disgrace.

95
00:08:13,630 --> 00:08:18,500
In the autumn of that same year, around the
festival of Mehregan, Bu Nasr was

96
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:23,370
wounded in a different way. Not by an enemy
army, but by his rivals at court.

97
00:08:24,150 --> 00:08:28,965
A list was drawn up of officials who would
have to supply horses and camels for the

98
00:08:28,965 --> 00:08:33,837
war. Beyhaqi tells us that Bu Nasr's name was
put on it, and he believed it was done

99
00:08:33,837 --> 00:08:38,130
on purpose, to provoke him, because everyone
knew he would not keep quiet.

100
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:43,560
He did not keep quiet. He sent the sultan a
sharp message. In Beyhaqi's words,

101
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:48,567
prison, disgrace, poverty and even death had
become sweet to him, compared with this

102
00:08:48,567 --> 00:08:53,157
humiliation. Coming from the most careful man
at court, those words must have

103
00:08:53,157 --> 00:08:54,170
shocked everyone.

104
00:08:54,950 --> 00:08:59,655
Masud was angry, but not with Bu Nasr. Beyhaqi
reports that he said the fault was

105
00:08:59,655 --> 00:09:04,534
not his secretary's, and he made peace with
him, and honored him again at his table.

106
00:09:04,534 --> 00:09:07,090
For a while, it seemed the storm had passed.

107
00:09:22,370 --> 00:09:27,189
It had not. Not long after, on a very cold
day, Bu Nasr carried letters in to the

108
00:09:27,189 --> 00:09:31,889
sultan and back out again, as he had done for
nearly thirty years. Then, in the

109
00:09:31,889 --> 00:09:34,090
garden by the chancery, he collapsed.

110
00:09:34,870 --> 00:09:39,570
Beyhaqi describes it as a stroke, coming in
waves, one after another, within an

111
00:09:39,570 --> 00:09:44,567
hour. His face twisted, and his body would not
obey him. A physician was brought. He

112
00:09:44,567 --> 00:09:46,590
said there was nothing to be done.

113
00:09:47,370 --> 00:09:52,081
When the news reached Masud, Beyhaqi tells us,
the sultan cried out, Alas for Bu

114
00:09:52,081 --> 00:09:56,734
Nasr. Great men of the court wept. Bu Nasr was
carried home, and soon after, he

115
00:09:56,734 --> 00:10:01,622
died. It was the year ten thirty nine, or in
the Islamic calendar, four hundred and

116
00:10:01,622 --> 00:10:02,270
thirty one.

117
00:10:03,050 --> 00:10:07,625
Now here, Beyhaqi does something that shows us
exactly why we can trust him. He

118
00:10:07,625 --> 00:10:12,315
reports a rumor. Some people said that the day
before, at a garden party, Bu Nasr

119
00:10:12,315 --> 00:10:17,179
had been given drinks that did him harm. A
lesser writer would have turned this into

120
00:10:17,179 --> 00:10:19,090
a murder story. Beyhaqi does not.

121
00:10:19,370 --> 00:10:24,387
He tells us what people said, and then he
says, in effect, that he will not judge.

122
00:10:24,387 --> 00:10:25,550
He leaves it there.

123
00:10:26,330 --> 00:10:30,958
That is the honest historian at work. He loved
this man. He had every reason to

124
00:10:30,958 --> 00:10:34,590
blame someone. And still he would not write
more than he knew.

125
00:10:49,870 --> 00:10:54,603
What did Bu Nasr leave behind? Beyhaqi tells
us that before he died, his master had

126
00:10:54,603 --> 00:10:59,221
asked the sultan to look after him, his pupil.
And when the chancery needed a new

127
00:10:59,221 --> 00:11:03,783
head, Masud said privately that if Beyhaqi had
not been so young, the post would

128
00:11:03,783 --> 00:11:05,950
have been his. He was about forty six.

129
00:11:06,230 --> 00:11:09,350
At that court, apparently, that was still
young.

130
00:11:10,130 --> 00:11:15,282
Instead, the job went to Bu Sahl Zawzani, the
same rival who had once offered to

131
00:11:15,282 --> 00:11:20,498
squeeze the old man for three hundred thousand
dinars. Beyhaqi became his deputy.

132
00:11:20,498 --> 00:11:22,430
You can imagine how that felt.

133
00:11:23,210 --> 00:11:28,029
And Beyhaqi wrote his master an epitaph in
Arabic, which means, roughly, that with

134
00:11:28,029 --> 00:11:32,555
him, ability, eloquence and good sense came to
an end. He also says something

135
00:11:32,555 --> 00:11:37,315
sadder. For thirty years, he writes, his
master did not have a single truly happy

136
00:11:37,315 --> 00:11:37,550
day.

137
00:11:38,330 --> 00:11:43,374
A man at the very top of the government,
trusted by two sultans, honored, rich. And

138
00:11:43,374 --> 00:11:47,750
not one happy day. That is the price of
standing so close to the throne.

139
00:12:03,030 --> 00:12:07,720
So, how does a secretary survive at a
dangerous court? Bu Nasr's answer seems to be:

140
00:12:07,720 --> 00:12:12,354
speak rarely, speak carefully, and when you
finally speak, tell the truth. And when

141
00:12:12,354 --> 00:12:16,430
you cannot change what is happening, at least
do not pretend it is right.

142
00:12:17,210 --> 00:12:22,308
If there is one lesson here, it is this: you
may not be able to stop an injustice,

143
00:12:22,308 --> 00:12:24,670
but you can refuse to call it justice.

144
00:12:39,950 --> 00:12:45,123
Next week, we go hunting with Sultan Masud,
and meet a lion. It is one of the most

145
00:12:45,123 --> 00:12:50,296
famous scenes in Beyhaqi, and it tells us a
great deal about the kind of man Masud

146
00:12:50,296 --> 00:12:50,990
really was.

147
00:12:51,770 --> 00:12:56,518
If you enjoyed this story, follow the show so
you don't miss the next one, and share

148
00:12:56,518 --> 00:13:01,154
it with someone who loves history. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani. This episode was retold from

149
00:13:01,154 --> 00:13:05,902
the Persian original of the History of
Beyhaqi, and narrated with an AI rendering of

150
00:13:05,902 --> 00:13:08,050
my own voice. Thank you for listening.

